**Why a question is missed (M3), test part**

| Set | Ranking | Missed | Duplication | Fusion | Rescoring | Ranking 11-100 | Vocabulary | Room hit, door missed |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Rust book | KAPU 0.2.1 | 23 | 3 | 5 | 1 | 10 | 4 | 1 |
| Rust book | KAPU, collapsed (0.3.0) | 20 | 0 | 5 | 1 | 10 | 4 | 8 |
| Kubernetes | KAPU 0.2.1 | 71 | 4 | 11 | 6 | 15 | 35 | 9 |
| Kubernetes | KAPU, collapsed (0.3.0) | 67 | 0 | 11 | 6 | 15 | 35 | 26 |
| MDN | KAPU 0.2.1 | 172 | 4 | 17 | 15 | 24 | 112 | 8 |
| MDN | KAPU, collapsed (0.3.0) | 168 | 0 | 17 | 15 | 24 | 112 | 23 |
| Skills, English | KAPU 0.2.1 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 2 |
| Skills, English | KAPU, collapsed (0.3.0) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 15 |
| Skills, translated | KAPU 0.2.1 | 7 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 7 | 1 |
| Skills, translated | KAPU, collapsed (0.3.0) | 7 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 7 | 7 |
| Skills, Hungarian | KAPU 0.2.1 | 16 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 4 | 10 | 2 |
| Skills, Hungarian | KAPU, collapsed (0.3.0) | 15 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 4 | 10 | 7 |
| PLC manuals | KAPU 0.2.1 | 4 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 |
| PLC manuals | KAPU, collapsed (0.3.0) | 3 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 21 |

- **The deduplicated chunk baselines are still better than KAPU, now on fair terms.** Chunk BM25 beats it on Kubernetes (0.484 against 0.451) and MDN (0.322 against 0.288), and the chunk hybrid is ahead everywhere except PLC. KAPU 0.3.0 reaches chunk BM25 on the Rust book (0.512) and the chunk hybrid on PLC (0.938).
- **Ceiling (recall@100):** the Rust book reaches 0.90 at 100 but 0.51 at 10, so its misses are mostly ranking. MDN reaches only 0.53 even at 100, so half of its questions use words its pages do not.
- **Error classes (M3), MDN with the collapse:** 112 of 168 misses are vocabulary (the page is not in any top 100), 24 ranking, 17 fusion, 15 rescoring candidates. Duplication is gone after the collapse. This points at document and query expansion (J7, J8) and smaller vector windows (J3), the next phases of the plan, not at more fusion tuning.
- **The cost of the collapse:** when a whole room outranks its own sections, the hit is the room, and the section that answers is only named under it. The last column grows: English skills 2 → 15, PLC 1 → 21 questions whose page is found but whose expected section is not a hit. On this repository's documentation, "how do I connect it to an agent" now returns the 5,497-token README room first and names its 663-token "Connect an agent" section under it. An agent that opens the hit reads more than it needs; `kapu_ask` opens the start of the room. Preferring the best section as the hit when it is nearly as good as its room is the next candidate, to be measured on the strict metrics (A57).

### kapu_ask (J12, D159) and the two-line excerpt (J11)

"Answered" means that a frame of the expected page is among the frames the one reply opens (`ask_eval.py`, test part, default budget 2,000 tokens).

| Set | 0.2.1 (best hit) | 0.3.0 (start of 5 rooms) | Reply tokens, 0.2.1 → 0.3.0 | Tokens per question spent without the page |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Rust book | 0.146 | 0.415 | 1,653 → 1,224 | 1,315 → 710 |
| Kubernetes | 0.172 | 0.393 | 1,800 → 1,130 | 1,391 → 678 |
| MDN | 0.114 | 0.216 | 1,614 → 1,124 | 1,331 → 854 |
| Skills, English | 0.826 | 0.957 | 1,788 → 1,145 | 295 → 51 |
| Skills, translated | 0.762 | 0.810 | 1,684 → 1,144 | 384 → 210 |

- **The shared budget wins everywhere.** The paired interval is above zero on every set except the translated one, and the reply is about a third smaller. The start of a room may not hold the answer line, so "answered" is lenient; the agent measurement below checks the answers themselves.
- **The confidence mode was the plan's proposal:** the best hit alone when it is found by both rankings and scores at least 1.3 times the second, otherwise the spread. It is not better on the dev part, and its signal is not calibrated: where it would say `confidence: high`, the expected room is there for only 11% of the Kubernetes and MDN questions, fewer than when it says low (42% and 22%). So 0.3.0 always spreads and prints no confidence line. The mode stays measurable (`kapu-mcp --rank '{"ask": "confidence"}'`).
- **The two-line excerpt (J11) costs hits and shows no gain** that this measurement can see. With it, 5.9 to 7.8 hits fit in the 600-token search reply instead of 7.2 to 9.5, and no answer quality was measured. It stays off (`excerpt_lines`).
- **The collapse in the search reply:** the line naming a room's other matching sections takes tokens, so 7.8 to 9.5 hits fit instead of 9.8 to 10. Every hit is now a different room.

### Search or reading: oracle, KAPU and closed book (N1), and abstention (N4)

On the PLC corpus, with Claude Sonnet through `claude -p` (isolated, no user settings) and Claude Haiku as the judge (`oracle_eval.py`, one run per question):
- 36 answerable questions, 6 per vendor;
- the 24 absent ones.

| | Correct | Partly | Wrong | Said not found | Input tokens (median) | $ (median) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Oracle: the expected page in the prompt | 97% | 3% | 0% | 0% | 5,461 | 0.015 |
| **KAPU tools** | **92%** | 8% | 0% | 0% | 12,892 | 0.019 |
| Closed book: the question alone | 28% | 17% | 8% | 47% | 2,906 | 0.006 |

| Absent questions | Said it is not there | Made an answer up | Input tokens (median) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| **KAPU tools** | **96%** | 4% (1 of 24) | 57,346 |
| Closed book | 54% | 46% | 2,895 |

- **Reading, not search, is the small loss here:** `retrieval_gap` (oracle - KAPU) is 0.06, `knowledge_gain` (KAPU - closed book) 0.64. The agent opened the expected room for 94% of the questions. Search is easy on this set, as noted above.
- **Abstention is KAPU's clearest win:** asked about something the manuals do not say, the model alone made an answer up for almost half of the questions, the KAPU agent for one of 24. A "not found" costs more, a median of 5.5 calls and 57,000 tokens, because the agent keeps searching.
- The confidence mode gave the same numbers (92% and 89% correct, 96% abstention), so `confidence: low` did not reduce made-up answers.
- **Limits:** one run per question; the plan asks for at least three (M5). The question writer saw the pages.

### The graph: followed neighbours (N2a) and topic groups (N13)

- **Neighbour lists are rarely followed.** In the logged live runs of chapters 5 and 8 (72 runs, `neighbour_follow.py`, the replies asked again from today's server), 5 of 27 later `kapu_open` calls opened a door from an earlier neighbour list: `link` 2 of 64 shown, `sim` 3 of 64, `term` 0 of 221. A neighbour list costs 70 tokens per opened door on average. This is the data for J16 (phase 4): `term` neighbours can go.

| Set | Rooms | Groups / folders | ARI | NMI (random same sizes) | k-means ARI | sim only | term only | link only | Largest group (rooms / tokens) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Kubernetes | 1,722 | 104 / 73 | 0.159 | 0.416 (0.150) | 0.137 | 0.155 | -0.005 | 0.000 | 166 / 1,509,243 |
| MDN | 14,719 | 99 / 50 | 0.040 | 0.390 (0.029) | 0.032 | 0.041 | -0.001 | -0.000 | 733 / 1,296,318 |

- **The groups carry structure, and the `sim` edges carry it.** Against the second folder level, the Kubernetes groups score ARI 0.159 and NMI 0.416 (random groups of the same sizes 0.150), about k-means on the room vectors (0.137). On MDN they score ARI 0.040 and NMI 0.390, against 0.029 for random groups and 0.032 for k-means.
- Leiden on one edge type at a time: `sim` alone gives what all edges give, `term` alone is at or below zero, and `link` edges are too few to form groups.
- The largest group is 166 rooms (1.5 M tokens) on Kubernetes and 733 rooms (1.3 M tokens) on MDN, which is why a group list does not fit a map reply (G16, J15).

### Found on the way
- **A memory bound (D157).** The float windows the vector rescoring keeps were bounded by doors only (20,000). The sign bits pick long doors with many windows, so on MDN a long run grew past 20 GB. They are now also bounded by windows (250,000, about 260 MB); a `kapu-mcp` server stays near 850 MB over 200 MDN searches.
- **Open (A55):** `kapu bench --config` keeps about 2 MB per run on a large file, so `ablation.py run --chunk 50` runs MDN in parts.
- **Two import problems** showed up while building the PLC corpus:
  - a file name longer than 64 characters gives an invalid room id (E008);
  - a control character left by PDF conversion stops the import (E004).

  The corpus was cleaned first; the import itself is unchanged.

### The tool list

With the new `kapu_search` and `kapu_ask` descriptions the six tools cost 799 tokens (K-01: at most 800); the first wording was 802, and a test now prices the list.

### Not done yet

These are phases 4 to 7 of the plan: J2c, J3, J5, J6, J7, J8, J13 to J16, M4, N3, N5 to N12.

## 13. Plan v3: a contextual model in plain Rust (0.5.0)

Measured on 2026-10-28 with tools 0.5.0 (specification v1.3, file format 1.3), on the machine of chapter 12, after the user's plan v3 ([results/2026-10-28-plan-v3.json](results/2026-10-28-plan-v3.json); per-question ranks in [results/2026-10-28-plan-v3-ranks.json](results/2026-10-28-plan-v3-ranks.json), by question hash). The method is that of chapter 12: seven question sets, the fixed dev/test split, the test part reported, a paired bootstrap with 10,000 draws, and the M0 rule.

**What was tried before the model.** Passage BM25 with a heading prefix, room-level fusion and a vector prefix (D168), and pseudo-relevance feedback (D169) are switches, off by default: none passes M0. Passage BM25 with English stemming brings KAPU's BM25 to the level of chunked BM25 (Rust book 0.390 to 0.537, Kubernetes 0.443 to 0.492), but the hybrid does not improve, because the static model's vectors limit it.

### The candidates on the same chunks (V1, D170)

Three models on the 512-token chunks of chapter 12, in Python: the vectors alone, the chunked hybrid, and KAPU's own BM25 ranking fused with the model's vectors at room level (what a model swap in KAPU could give). Difference to the packaged static model on the test part, pooled:

| Model | Ranking | Δ recall@10 [95%] | Δ MRR@10 [95%] |
|---|---|---:|---:|
| potion-retrieval-32M | vectors alone | +0.021 [-0.017, +0.059] | +0.016 [-0.010, +0.042] |
| potion-retrieval-32M | chunked hybrid | -0.007 [-0.033, +0.019] | +0.002 [-0.019, +0.023] |
| potion-retrieval-32M | KAPU BM25 + vectors | +0.003 [-0.026, +0.033] | +0.009 [-0.013, +0.032] |
| multilingual-e5-small-qint8 | vectors alone | +0.123 [+0.083, +0.163] | +0.072 [+0.042, +0.102] |
| multilingual-e5-small-qint8 | chunked hybrid | +0.064 [+0.036, +0.092] | +0.039 [+0.016, +0.062] |
| multilingual-e5-small-qint8 | KAPU BM25 + vectors | +0.071 [+0.040, +0.102] | +0.039 [+0.016, +0.063] |

`potion-retrieval-32M` helps in English but breaks down on Hungarian questions (its vocabulary is English only). `multilingual-e5-small` is the first change of the plan whose pooled interval is above zero in both metrics.

### The encoder (V2, D171)

The user chose a pure Rust runtime with nothing more to download. `multilingual-e5-small` in the int8 form of ONNX Runtime's dynamic quantization uses only standard operations, so KAPU runs it with its own code (`crates/kapu-core/src/bert.rs`), with no new dependency: every matrix product is exact in integers, every float sum has a fixed order, and `exp` and `erf` are fixed formulas. A vector therefore has the same bits on SSE2, AVX2 and AVX-512 builds (a golden test, also run by the release build on every platform), and the D46 rule stays. Agreement with ONNX Runtime: cosine 0.998 to 1.000 on short texts, 0.9993 on 512 tokens; every activation is rounded to 8 bits, so one last-bit difference can move a value to the next step, and ONNX Runtime differs as much between machines. One 512-token window takes 0.45 s on one thread built for AVX-512, 1.3 s with the baseline (SSE2) build.

### Storing the vectors

A transformer's windows cannot be computed when a file is read (MDN would take half an hour), so they are stored. The storage forms simulated on the chunk vectors, KAPU BM25 + vectors, difference to the float vectors on the test part:

| Form | Bytes per window | Δ recall@10 [95%] |
|---|---:|---:|
| float, centred | 1536 | -0.021 [-0.042, +0.000] |
| sign bits, the best 200 by 8 bits | 432 | -0.121 [-0.154, -0.088] |
| 8 bits | 384 | +0.002 [+0.000, +0.005] |
| sign bits, the best 200 by 4 bits | 240 | -0.127 [-0.159, -0.095] |
| centred sign bits, then 4 bits | 240 | -0.012 [-0.033, +0.009] |
| 4 bits of the residual from the mean | 194 | -0.009 [-0.023, +0.005] |
| 4 bits | 192 | -0.012 [-0.028, +0.003] |
| 4 bits, centred | 192 | -0.017 [-0.038, +0.003] |
| 3 steps, centred | 144 | -0.019 [-0.040, +0.002] |
| -1, 0, +1 of the residual | 98 | -0.035 [-0.055, -0.014] |
| -1, 0, +1, centred | 96 | -0.021 [-0.043, +0.002] |
| sign bits of the residual | 50 | -0.080 [-0.109, -0.052] |
| sign bits | 48 | -0.203 [-0.239, -0.168] |
| sign bits, centred | 48 | -0.033 [-0.055, -0.010] |

The sign bits that carry the static model (D143) lose 0.20 with this model: its vectors share one direction. In KAPU itself 4 bits measured as good as 8, so a contextual model stores 4 bits a component (`#VEC` `bits=4`, format 1.3); the reader scores every window from the file, with no rescoring. A room with sub-doors has windows of its own text only, so no text is computed twice. The build computes on every core and keeps each window in a log in the cache folder: an interrupted build goes on where it stopped, and a rebuild computes only new windows (Kubernetes 12 s instead of 15 minutes).

### In KAPU's own search

The set files rebuilt with the model, `kapu bench`, against the static model of 0.3 (default ranking), test part pooled:

| Model | Bits | Ranking | Δ recall@10 [95%] | Δ MRR@10 [95%] | Worst set | p50 ratio |
|---|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| multilingual-e5-small | 8 | default ranking | +0.062 [+0.035, +0.090] | +0.027 [+0.005, +0.049] | -0.065 | 14.9 |
| multilingual-e5-small | 4 | default ranking | +0.062 [+0.035, +0.090] | +0.028 [+0.006, +0.052] | -0.056 | 14.9 |
| multilingual-e5-small | 4 | passage BM25 | +0.069 [+0.038, +0.101] | +0.038 [+0.014, +0.062] | -0.070 | 14.6 |
| multilingual-e5-small | 4 | passage BM25 and room fusion | +0.075 [+0.045, +0.106] | +0.038 [+0.014, +0.063] | -0.085 | 14.5 |

Per set, default ranking, 4 bits (search time: median of `kapu bench`, the query's vector built for AVX-512, and with the baseline SSE2 build of the release):

| Set | Test questions | recall@10, 0.3 | recall@10, e5 | MRR@10, 0.3 | MRR@10, e5 | Search, 0.3 | e5 | e5, SSE2 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Rust book | 41 | 0.512 | 0.585 | 0.252 | 0.263 | 0.8 ms | 12.6 ms | 35.0 ms |
| Kubernetes | 122 | 0.451 | 0.582 | 0.253 | 0.271 | 27.1 ms | 25.7 ms | 48.4 ms |
| MDN | 236 | 0.288 | 0.360 | 0.135 | 0.195 | 408.5 ms | 155.9 ms | 320.4 ms |
| Skills, English | 46 | 0.978 | 0.978 | 0.853 | 0.868 | 10.1 ms | 17.2 ms | 43.1 ms |
| Skills, translated | 42 | 0.833 | 0.833 | 0.759 | 0.770 | 12.7 ms | 17.6 ms | - |
| Skills, Hungarian | 42 | 0.643 | 0.643 | 0.492 | 0.436 | 10.7 ms | 18.9 ms | - |
| PLC manuals | 48 | 0.938 | 0.938 | 0.734 | 0.751 | 29.7 ms | 30.1 ms | - |

| File | Today | With e5, 4 bits | Growth | With 8 bits | First build |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Rust book | 2.4 MB | 2.6 MB | +9% | +20% | 71 s |
| Kubernetes | 28.1 MB | 31.4 MB | +12% | +25% | 922 s |
| MDN | 139.0 MB | 160.7 MB | +16% | +34% | 2986 s |
| Skills, English | 5.9 MB | 6.5 MB | +10% | +21% | 124 s |
| PLC manuals | 12.3 MB | 13.4 MB | +9% | +20% | 321 s |

- **The model is the lever.** Recall@10 +0.062 with the default ranking, +0.075 with passage BM25 and room fusion, both intervals above zero; Kubernetes 0.451 to 0.582, MDN 0.288 to 0.360, the exact PLC section 0.500 to 0.812.
- **It does not pass M0.** Hungarian questions on the English skills lose MRR (0.492 to 0.436: in this language pair the small e5 is weaker than the static model, as the chunk measurement showed), and a small file is searched about 12 ms (AVX-512) to 35 ms (SSE2) slower, the query's vector. A large file is faster: MDN 408 to 156 ms (AVX-512), 320 ms (SSE2), because nothing is rescored. The user accepted the trade (D172): 0.5.0 ships the model, the ranking switches stay off.
- **The strict metric** falls on the Stack Overflow sets (Rust book 0.317 to 0.049), because the vectors now give a section as the hit, while there the expected hit is the whole page (chapter 12); on the PLC manuals, which expect the section, it rises.
- **With the same model** the chunked hybrid is 0.585 on the Rust book, 0.533 on Kubernetes and 0.449 on MDN (chunks of chapter 12): level with KAPU, behind it and ahead of it. KAPU's advantage stays the section hit with its token price, in one file with no server.
- **The file grows by 9-16%**, above the 3% of K-11, which the user accepted for the model; the 8-bit form would be 20-34%.


## 14. A small local model: qwen3:8b with KAPU (J2)

Measured from 2026-10-28 to 2026-11-05 with `qwen3:8b` on an 8 GB GPU (Ollama; at 32k context about 40% of the model runs on the CPU), through the Vellum client and its KAPU branch (`feat/kapu-knowledge`, 028d7e6: `--kapu-dir`, `--kapu-rank`; 815c6a9 for 0.6.0: `--fresh-server`), the way a user runs it; one run per question, temperature 0 ([results/2026-11-02-j2-qwen.json](results/2026-11-02-j2-qwen.json), with 0.6.0 [results/2026-11-05-j2-qwen.json](results/2026-11-05-j2-qwen.json)). The judge is Claude Haiku; on the PLC manuals with the judge prompts of chapter 12 (N1) word for word, and Claude's stored answers are scored again by the same judge. Tools 0.3.0 with the static model, and 0.5.0 with e5 (the PLC file rebuilt in 13.5 minutes); 0.5.1 gives the same vectors, so its numbers are those of 0.5.0. And 0.6.0, with the programs of the release, the e5 files and every question in its own `kapu-mcp` session, because within one session `kapu_ask` does not repeat a passage (D176): a client that keeps one server through its conversations gets less from it than these numbers.

The forms: closed book; KAPU tools (the model calls them); KAPU prefetch and tools (the client first puts a `kapu_ask` reply into the prompt, within its knowledge budget: 600 tokens at 8k context and 2000 at 32k in the runs of 0.3 and 0.5, unless the table says otherwise; 600 at 8k, 4000 at 16k and 8000 at 32k, Vellum's table since these runs, with 0.6); a Markdown folder with grep and line reads; the whole file pasted, cut to fit; and the oracle, the right page in the prompt.

