| Kubernetes (30) | 10% / 70% / 17% | - | 13% / 80% / 3% | 10% / 80% / 7% | 7% / 37% / 20% |
| MDN (30) | 30% / 63% / 3% | - | 20% / 63% / 3% | 30% / 57% / 3% | 33% / 30% / 10% |
| PixelLab (12) | 0% / 25% / 67% | 8% / 42% / 8% | 0% / 50% / 42% | 0% / 75% / 25% | 0% / 25% / 0% |

- **0.6.0 moves the small model on manuals it cannot know.** At 8k context, on the same card, 1 of 36 fully correct alone, 0 with grep, 2 with 0.5 and 12 with 0.6 (tools alone 10); at 16k 17, at 32k 21, where 0.5 had 16 with 6000 tokens. The right room is read for 81% of the questions at 32k. Paired sign tests on the 36 questions: 0.6 at 8k against the model alone 12 and 1 (p = 0.003), against 0.5 12 and 2 (p = 0.013), 32k against 8k 11 and 2 (p = 0.02); 0.6 against 0.5 at 32k 8 and 3 (p = 0.23), not a measured difference. What changed is what the reply holds: at 600 tokens two passages with the evidence instead of the opening lines of two rooms. It answers less often that it did not find it (at 8k 39% instead of 50%).
- **It makes up less than alone, and more than 0.5.** Of the 24 questions the manuals do not answer it makes up 17 alone, 1 with 0.5 at 8k, 4 with 0.6 at 8k, 7 at 16k and 6 at 32k. Against the model alone that is a measured difference (17 to 4: 13 and 0, p = 0.0002), from 0.5 to 0.6 it is not on this few questions (3 and 0, p = 0.25), but all three contexts lean the same way: passages next to the question invite an answer. The tools alone at 8k make up 1, with 10 fully correct.
- **The gap is still reading, not the knowledge.** With the right page in the prompt the small model answers 75-81% of the PLC questions correctly; with KAPU 0-58%, while Claude with KAPU answers 92%.
- **The budget counts with the context.** At 32k context a knowledge budget of 2000 tokens gives 11% correct, 6000 tokens 39% and 12,000 tokens 44% (and 19% partly). Vellum's table now gives 4000 at 16k and 8000 at 32k, the 0.6 rows above; with 0.6 the reply fills such a budget with passages (2 at 600 tokens, 13 at 4000, 25 at 8000 on this file).
- **KAPU stops the guessing.** Closed book the small model makes up an answer to 71% of the questions the manuals do not answer; with KAPU 0-29%.
- **0.5 finds the right room more often** (53% to 58-64% at 8k, 67% at 32k) and makes up less at 8k (21% to 4%); at 32k with 6000 tokens 44% instead of 39% correct. `kapu_ask` giving the best door whole instead of sharing the budget: 5 of 36 correct instead of 1 at 8k (both with passage BM25). These are 36 questions and one run: a direction, not a measured difference.
- **Elsewhere, up to 0.5:** on the Rust book the tools help (17% to 37% correct, 3% wrong), the prefetch at 8k does not (13%; its prompt is 6,600 tokens instead of 2,500); on Kubernetes and MDN KAPU does not help the small model (the right page is read for 33% and 7% of the questions); on PixelLab nothing is correct in any form, but KAPU cuts the wrong answers from 67% to 17-33%, and 32k does not change that. The whole file pasted or a Markdown folder with grep does not work for the small model (PLC 3% and 0% correct; with grep 69% "not in the documents").
- **Elsewhere, with 0.6:** where the model half knows the subject from training, KAPU changes little. Fully correct on the Rust book 17% alone and 13-17% with KAPU, Kubernetes 10% and 10-13%, MDN 30% and 20-30%; the wrong answers fall on the Rust book (13% to 0-3%) and on Kubernetes (17% to 3-7%). The tools alone on the Rust book went down from 37% with 0.3 to 13% with 0.6 (one run on 30 questions, no cause looked for). On MDN the client's router asks the file for a third of the questions, and the right page is read for 7-20%. On PixelLab 1 of 12 at best in any form, and the wrong answers go from 67% to 8-42%. Grep over the Markdown reaches nothing at 32k either: 0 of 36 on PLC, 86% "not found".
- **Cost** on the 8 GB card: 12-15 s a question at 8k, 29-40 s at 16k, 60-75 s at 32k on PLC. With 0.6 a full budget at 32k takes longer where the question is long: a median of 157 s on the Rust book and 103 s on Kubernetes. One long Rust question got no token within the client's three-minute limit, on three attempts, so that row has 29 questions.

## 15. One Markdown file with grep against KAPU (plan v4 and v5)

Measured from 2026-11-02 to 2026-11-04 with `tools/study/md_eval.py`: Claude Sonnet through `claude -p`, judge Haiku, no user settings, one unrecorded call first that warms the prompt cache. The PLC manuals of chapter 12 as one KAPU file and as one Markdown file; 36 answerable questions, 24 whose answer is not in the manuals. The PixelLab documentation is third-party and not published. The manuals and the raw runs are not in this repository.

**Each question on its own, three runs:**

| Form | Dollars for 60 questions (range) | First run | Fully correct of 108 | Partly | Made up of 72 | Calls: answerable, absent |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| One Markdown file, Grep and Read | 1.85 (1.64-2.02) | 2.02 | 99 | 9 | 5 | 2.54, 8.22 |
| KAPU 0.5.1 | 2.56 (2.40-2.66) | 2.61 | 99 | 9 | 2 | 2.56, 6.01 |
| **KAPU 0.6.0** | **1.42 (1.33-1.50)** | **1.50** | 98 | 9 | 2 | 1.59, 5.17 |

The first run is the fresh price: in the second and third run the same prompt is already in the prompt cache (a KAPU answer with one call costs 0.0138 dollars in the first run, 0.0100 after it). The ratio hardly changes: 74% on first runs, 77% on the means. On 12 requests about a pixel-art tool, three runs: 0.23 dollars against 0.39, with 5 and 7 of 36 fully correct, 28 and 27 partly, 3 and 2 wrong; that judge is noisy.

**The hook and the limit, one run each (first-run prices, plan v5):**

| Form | Dollars | Of the Markdown file | Fully correct of 36 | Made up of 24 | Calls: answerable, absent |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| One Markdown file, Grep and Read | 2.02 | 100% | 32 | 1 | 2.50, 8.62 |
| KAPU 0.6.0, tools alone | 1.50 | 74% | 31 | 1 | 1.64, 4.71 |
| The hook (FR-21) | 1.53 | 76% | 33 | 1 | 0.94, 4.29 |
| The hook with its stop line | 1.52 | 75% | 35 | 1 | 1.11, 4.08 |
| The hook and at most one tool call (left out) | 0.90 | 44% | 24 | 0 | 0.69, 1.42 |

- **What costs money is new text, not rounds.** The dollars `claude` reports fit exactly, on 600 calls: input 2, cache write 4, cache read 0.1, output 10 dollars per million tokens. The hook saves a round, but the passages have to be written either way, on the absent questions too. An answer without a tool call costs 0.0118 dollars, one with one call in a fresh run 0.0138.
- **The agent does not stop when asked to.** With a line that says when to stop, the absent questions went from 4.29 to 4.08 calls.
- **A hard limit stops it, at the price of accuracy.** With one tool call per question all 24 absent questions were declined at 0.0163 dollars each (the Markdown file: 0.0546), but 8 answerable questions became a false "not found" and one wrong. In 13 of the 15 questions that used a tool the agent spent its call on `kapu_grep`, as its lead line suggested. At 5 of those 8 the evidence, by word overlap, was already in the passages. The limit is not in the code.
- **The passages cannot simply shrink.** Without an agent: evidence for 29 of 36 at 1,000 tokens, 22 at 800, 21 at 700 and 600, 17 at 500.

**Sessions, one run each:**

| Questions one after the other | Dollars | Of the Markdown file | Calls | Fully correct | Partly | Not found | Wrong |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 25 PLC: one Markdown file | 0.72 | 100% | 70 | 19 of 20 | 0 | 1 | 0 |
| 25 PLC: KAPU 0.5.1 | 1.20 | 167% | 64 | 19 of 20 | 1 | 0 | 0 |
| **25 PLC: KAPU 0.6.0** | **0.58** | **81%** | 38 | 17 of 20 | 2 | 1 | 0 |
| 12 pixel-art: one Markdown file | 0.52 | 100% | 33 | 2 of 12 | 9 | 0 | 1 |
| 12 pixel-art: KAPU 0.5.1 | 0.42 | 82% | 26 | 4 of 12 | 6 | 0 | 2 |
| **12 pixel-art: KAPU 0.6.0 before the passage tail** | **0.26** | **50%** | 15 | 4 of 12 | 8 | 0 | 0 |
| 12 pixel-art: the whole Markdown file pasted | 1.68 | 324% | 0 | 2 of 12 | 10 | 0 | 0 |

All 5 absent PLC questions were declined by all three forms. One run each cannot tell whether 17 and 19 of 20 differ. `claude --resume` reports the session's running total, so a session's dollars are those of its last turn.

## 16. Reproduce

```bash
python tools/collect_skills.py bench/korpusz/rooms           # the installed skills as rooms (local only)
kapu import <rooms folder> corpus.kapu --model models/multilingual-e5-small-q8
kapu validate corpus.kapu --full
kapu bench corpus.kapu questions.jsonl --json                # recall@5/10, MRR@10 per mode
python tools/mcp_probe.py corpus.kapu questions.jsonl        # MCP latency and response sizes
python tools/kapu_mcp_call.py call kapu_search '{"file": "...", "query": "..."}'
```

Chapter 14: the Vellum client's KAPU branch (`feat/kapu-knowledge`, 028d7e6, `--kapu-dir` and `--kapu-rank`; 815c6a9 and `--fresh-server` for 0.6.0) with Ollama, and the scripts of the study folder (`prepare.py`, `run_*.sh`, `judge.py` with a logged-in `claude` CLI, `report.py`), which are not in this repository; `tools/study/collect_j2.py` writes the results file from the report. Chapter 13: `tools/study/model_candidates.py` (`run` per set, `@<form>` for the storage forms, `report`), `tools/onnx_to_kapu.py` (the model folder), `kapu --model <folder> build` on a copy of each set file, `ablation.py run` with the matrix `bench/ablation/v1.json` (`KAPU_MODEL_DIR` and `KAPU_CACHE_DIR` set), `ablation.py report` with `--base ml-base` on the runs of both models side by side, and `collect_v3.py` (the results file). Chapter 12: `tools/study/split_questions.py` (the dev/test split), `ablation.py run` and `report` (the matrices `bench/ablation/m0.json`, `m1.json`, `m2.json`; set `KAPU_MODEL_DIR` for a copied binary, `--chunk 50` for MDN), `chunk_baseline.py` (with `--chunks` in the report), `ask_eval.py` (`bench/ablation/ask-modes*.json`), `title_weight.py`, `neighbour_follow.py`, `group_quality.py`, `oracle_eval.py` (needs a logged-in `claude` CLI), `collect_v2.py` (the results file) and `v2_tables.py` (these tables). Chapter 11: `tools/study/skill_pool.py` collects the skills of the machine, `tools/study/skill_eval.py` prepares the three forms (`prepare`), runs the tasks of `tools/study/skill_tasks.json` (`run`), measures the fixed cost (`baseline`) and writes the results file (`report`); it needs a logged-in `claude` CLI. Chapter 10: `tools/study/measure_v12.py` (reply prices, `kapu_ask`, open times over MCP), `tools/study/measure_scale.py` (a file of a given size), `tools/study/translate_hu.py` (the translated questions) and `tools/study/collect_v12.py` (the results file). Chapters 8 and 9 (public documentation): `tools/study/prepare_corpus.py` and `so_questions.py` prepare the corpora and questions, `scale_probe.py` measures a size, `chunk_baseline.py` the chunk baselines, `collect_results.py` writes `results/2026-10-23-study-pilot.json`, and `results_page.py` turns that and the format 1.2 results file into the results page.

Chapter 15: `python tools/study/md_eval.py prepare`, `run` (`--forms kapu,md`, `kapu-hook` for the hook), `session`, `judge` and `report`; the usage is at the top of the script.

@@@ test-vectors
# Test vectors (M1)

Generated by `tools/build_vectors.py`; never edit these files by hand (some
contain a CR or a control byte on purpose, and `.gitattributes` marks them
binary so git leaves every byte alone). `expected.json` records what each file
must produce; the Rust tests in `crates/kapu-core/tests/acceptance.rs` read it.

| File | Expected | What it exercises |
|---|---|---|
| `minta.kapu` | valid | 3 rooms, 4 sub-doors, 2 `link` edges, `@@@` escapes (0 and 1 backslash), a `## ` line inside a code fence, a `\|` in a title, one room with and one without a trailing LF, the untouched `kapu new` template as room `szoba`. Built without a model (`emb=none`), so FR-10/4 applies to every reader. |
| `E001-magic.kapu` | E001 | first line `%KAPX 1.2` |
| `E002-header.kapu` | E002 | byte 511 is a space instead of LF |
| `E003-cr.kapu` | E003 | a CR inside `#MAP` (header recomputed, nothing else wrong) |
| `E004-control.kapu` | E004 | ESC (0x1B) inside `#DESC` |
| `E005-span.kapu` | E005 | `desc=` length overstated by 7 bytes, so it overlaps `#GRAPH` |
| `E006-bodyhash.kapu` | E006 | `bodyhash=` field wrong, body intact |
| `E007-doorhash.kapu` | E007 | one door's fingerprint altered in `#DOCS` |
| `E008-id.kapu` | E008 | room id `Szoba` (uppercase) used consistently in every section |
| `E009-dangling.kapu` | E009 | `#INDEX` posting that points past the last `#DOCS` line |
| `E010-caption.kapu` | E010 | 201-character title in `#MAP` |
| `E011-hashprefix.kapu` | E011 | `bodyhash=sha256:...` |
| `edge-stale-same-length.kapu` | reader: `index: stale` | room 1 edited in place with equal length: fingerprint mismatch, retry, in-memory rebuild, edited text served |
| `edge-stale-shifted.kapu` | reader: `index: stale` | room 1 grew by one line: later positions are off, rebuild from the `@@@` lines |

`expected.json` also lists every door of the sample with its id, title, price
(`o200k_base` tokens), character count, offset, length, BLAKE3 fingerprint,
group and served text, so M2 and M3 can assert against them directly.

`../compat/minta-1.0.kapu` and `../compat/minta-1.1.kapu` are the sample before
the `kind: note` line of format 1.2, in format 1.0 (postings by door id) and 1.1
(postings by gap, no `#VEC`); the reader must still read both (D140, D143).

The decisions that pin the open points of the spec for these vectors are
D56-D66; `tools/build_vectors.py` is their executable form.

